UID:dc312f28a6337739@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260908T210822Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Heads to The Big E with Food\, Farms\, 
 and Makers
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nDACF Home\n→\nAbout DACF\n→\nNewsroom\n→ News
 \nMaine Heads to The Big E with Food\, Farms\, and Makers\nSeptember 3\, 2
 026\nThe Maine Building at the Big E returns for 17 days of Maine food\, f
 arms\, makers\, and traditions\, September 18 - October 4\nAUGUSTA\, Maine
  (September 3\, 2026) - From loaded Maine baked potatoes and lobster rolls
  to wild blueberry crisp\, maple milkshakes\, smoked salmon\, and wood-fir
 ed pizza featuring Maine ingredients\, the tastes and traditions of Maine 
 are returning to\nThe Big E\nthis fall.\nThe State of Maine Building\, loc
 ated on the\nAvenue of States\nalongside buildings representing the five o
 ther New England states\, will welcome visitors throughout the 17-day East
 ern States Exposition\, September 18 through October 4\, 2026\, in West Sp
 ringfield\, Massachusetts. Maine Day will be celebrated on Saturday\, Sept
 ember 19.\nFor more than a century\, the Maine Building has introduced gen
 erations of fairgoers to the people\, products\, and traditions that make 
 Maine stand apart. The Maine Department of Agriculture\, Conservation and 
 Forestry (DACF) owns and operates the building as a showcase for Maine agr
 iculture\, food\, fisheries\, forests\, tourism\, artisans\, and businesse
 s.\n"The Big E gives Maine a special opportunity to showcase our food\, fa
 rms\, and agricultural businesses to people from across New England and be
 yond\,"\nsaid DACF Commissioner Amanda Beal\n. "Food is one of the most im
 mediate and memorable ways to make that connection. Someone may come into 
 the Maine Building for a baked potato\, lobster roll\, or a maple or wild 
 blueberry dish\, but they leave with a greater appreciation for the farmer
 s\, producers\, and small businesses behind those products."\nThe Big E is
  one of the largest fairs in North America\, providing Maine businesses wi
 th access to an enormous regional marketplace. For exhibiting producers an
 d makers\, the opp\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13
 362241
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13362241
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fc896e87e7306240@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260908T210759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Annual Tea Celebrating Deaf Culture to Take P
 lace September 17
DESCRIPTION:Skip First Level Navigation\nSkip All Navigation\nMaine.gov\nA
 gencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nMaine Departmen
 t of Labor\nContact Us\n|\nNews\n|\nOnline services\n|\nPublications\n|\nS
 itemap\nHome\nContact Us\nOnline Services\nAbout MDOL\nNews & Events\nForm
 s & Publications\nMDOL Bureaus\nHome\n→\nNews & Events\n→ Annual Tea C
 elebrating Deaf Culture to Take Place September 17\nServices\nJobs & Train
 ing\nUnemployment Insurance\nDisability Services\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor 
 Laws\nLabor Statistics\nWorkforce Development\nAnnual Tea Celebrating Deaf
  Culture to Take Place September 17\nSeptember 8\, 2026\nFOR IMMEDIATE REL
 EASE: September 8\, 2026\nContact: Terry Morrell 470-7668 (videophone)\; 7
 11 Maine Relay\nAnnual Tea Celebrating Deaf Culture to Take Place Septembe
 r 17\nThe 35th annual tea and awards presentation will take place from 2 t
 o 4 p.m. in the Hall of Flags\nAUGUSTA\, MAINE\n-In celebration of Deaf cu
 lture and the heritage of AmericanSign Language (ASL)\, as well as Deaf li
 terature\, art and folklore\, Governor Janet Mills has issued a\nproclamat
 ion\ndesignating the week of Sept. 20-26\, 2026\, as Deaf Culture Week.\nT
 o kick off the week\, the Commission for the Deaf\, Hard of Hearing and La
 te Deafened will hold its\n35th Annual Tea in Celebration of Deaf Culture 
 Week on Sept. 17\, from 2 to 4 p.m. at the Hall of Flags in the State Hous
 e in Augusta.\nThe event is open to the public.\nClayton Marr III will giv
 e the keynote address. Marr grew up in Westbrook and currently lives in Kn
 oxville\, Tenn. where he is the student activity coordinator for the\nTenn
 essee Schools for the Deaf\n. He works with Deaf students to support their
  academic success\, personal growth\, and independence.\n"Maine's Deaf com
 munity has enriched our state for generations through its language\, cultu
 re\, traditions and contributions. I've proudly declared September 20-26 a
 s Deaf Culture Week in celebration of that heritage and the Deaf and hard-
 of-hearing Mainers who make our state a better place\," said Governor Mill
 s.\n"The Maine Dep\nhttp://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?i
 d=13362246
URL:http://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?id=13362246
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
